odio-kiosk (2.0.0) trixie; urgency=medium

  Own source, Qt 6, renamed odio-kiosk

  * The browser source lives here now, reduced to the kiosk window: the
    Qt 5 simplebrowser example that upstream carries behind FULLBROWSER is
    gone, along with its tabs, downloads, popups and dialogs. What remains
    is main.cpp and mainwindow.cpp, derived from e1z0/Framebuffer-browser
    at 4213a69 and still LGPL-3.
  * Built against Qt 6 instead of Qt 5.15, so the engine is Chromium 122
    rather than Chromium 87 — five years of web platform, and the end of
    silently dropped CSS rules and missing JavaScript built-ins.
  * MainWindow no longer persists the current URL on close: it wrote
    through a QSettings pointer that was never allocated.
  * The package, the binary and the unit are odio-kiosk, which they did not
    agree on before. It Provides/Replaces/Breaks fbrowser-kiosk, so an
    installed node migrates on upgrade.
  * The unit is a template, odio-kiosk@.service: one instance per screen.
    /etc/default/odio-kiosk carries the node's Qt environment, and each
    screen names its page, and its framebuffer as FB if it is not the
    first, in ~/.config/odio-kiosk/<instance>.conf. The health check waits
    on the same URL. The unit names no server: ordering it after odio-api,
    and restarting it with odio-api, is a drop-in the deployment writes.
  * The user agent is Qt's own, which names QtWebEngine and its Chromium,
    with an odio-kiosk suffix. It used to claim to be Firefox 89, which
    made every server log line about this browser misleading.

 -- Mathieu Réquillart <mathieu.requillart@gmail.com>  Sun, 20 Sep 2026 15:00:00 +0200
